appen first) consequential to clinical options. [Data: Entities (2028); Relationships (3403)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Testing targets span DNA mutations/alterations, gene expression patterns, and protein levels—reflecting a multi-omic characterization approach\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community’s biomarker concept is not limited to single-gene mutation checks. BIOMARKER TESTING is described as evaluating molecular and cellular features such as DNA mutations/alterations, gene expression patterns, protein expression/levels, and receptor status, with results commonly reported by a pathology specialist. This explicitly broad scope supports both classification and therapy selection. [Data: Entities (1191, 1624, 1628, 1917, 5252); Relationships (4690, 4691, 11661, 11662)]\\n\\nPROTEIN LEVELS are specifically linked as a measurement that can be performed in tumor samples as part of molecular evaluation guiding therapy choices (with PD-L1 given as an illustrative example in the text). This creates a concrete bridge between “molecular testing” and immunotherapy-relevant protein biomarker assessment, complementing DNA-focused approaches. [Data: Entities (1628, 1191); Relationships (4690)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"NGS and CGP enable broad biomarker discovery in a single workflow, supporting actionable mutation and fusion detection\",\n      \"explanation\": \"NEXT-GENERATION SEQUENCING (NGS) appears as a core enabling technology for biomarker testing, described as a sequencing method that can be more comprehensive than other approaches and can identify clinically relevant alterations—including mutations and gene fusions—supporting therapy choice (including in recurrent/advanced/metastatic disease contexts such as thyroid cancer in the text). [Data: Entities (1850, 1625, 2165, 5253); Relationships (11667, 11368, 11369, 3085)]\\n\\nCOMPREHENSIVE GENOMIC PROFILING (CGP) is explicitly positioned as a broad molecular/genomic testing approach “wi