pend on tumor biology. This relationship highlights the strategic role of molecular characterization when treatment is systemic and choices among targeted and immune therapies are consequential. [Data: Entities (1586, 1191); Relationships (2570)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Clinical operations and interpretation roles matter: pathologists/hematopathologists, and variability across hospitals in available test menus\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The Pathologist / Hematopathologist is explicitly described as the physician specialist who examines/analyzes biopsied tissue to determine disease presence/type and provides diagnostic and staging information that guides clinical decisions. This role also supports biomarker testing by ensuring appropriate testing is performed and results are communicated in clinically meaningful terms. [Data: Entities (1917); Relationships (3239)]\\n\\nOperational constraints are also explicit: some HOSPITALS AND TREATMENT CENTERS may not provide the full range of biomarker tests, which can affect where testing is performed and implies that specimen routing (e.g., sending tissue elsewhere) may be necessary to obtain comprehensive results. This is a key community insight because capability heterogeneity can directly affect time-to-result and completeness of actionable findings. [Data: Entities (5576); Relationships (12462)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Limits and exceptions are noted: molecular testing may have reduced treatment value in mouth cancers due to few targeted drugs\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Not all cancer contexts derive equal benefit from molecular testing in terms of immediate treatment guidance. The relationships explicitly note that, for MOUTH CANCERS, molecular testing is discussed as having limited value for guiding treatment because there are relatively few targeted drugs for the relevant mutations. This is an important counterbalance to the otherwise broad “precision oncology” emphasis in the community